  "solution": "<p><strong>1. Find the stationary points.</strong> For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>5</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>10</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>120</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>&#x02032;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>15</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>30</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>120</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>15</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Its only real zeros are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>2. Establish the directions and heights.</strong> The function increases, then decreases, then increases across those boundaries. Its turning heights are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>254</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>194</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>3. Count exactly one crossing in each interval.</strong> On the left the graph increases from arbitrarily negative values to a positive height. In the middle it decreases from positive to negative. On the right it increases from a negative height to arbitrarily positive values. Continuity gives a crossing in each interval, and strict monotonicity allows no second crossing there.</p><p><strong>Answer C.</strong> There are 3 real roots.</p>",
